On May 29, 2:19 pm, Janis <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com> wrote:
> On 29 Mai, 12:00, James <james.ka...@gmail.com> wrote:

> > On May 28, 1:59 pm, Janis <janis_papanag...@hotmail.com> wrote:

> > There are at least two ways you can easily get a robe.  It's
> > rare for me to get beyond the castle without one.

> Only for very specific values of "easily", I'd say. :-)

:-)  Well, there's nothing easier than wishing for one, once
you've got the wand of wishing from the castle.  (Whether it
corresponds to your priorities at the moment is another
question.)  I also find that post-Sobokan, I tend to have a lot
of tripe which I don't really know what to do with.  In recent
games, I've found it effective to convert the tripe into pets,
once I've found a polymorph trap (and there's always a couple of
those), and as I said somewhere else: pet+polymorph trap+priest
=3D robe.  (Not always, but fairly often.  And by that time,
there's usually a priest available that I can spare.)

> > (But there are always trade offs involved.  A robe means
> > GDSM, which means that reflection must come from somewhere
> > else.  And of course, a Samurai doesn't want a shield, so
> > choosing a robe ends up meaning no amulet of life saving.)

> Not sure why you mention AoLS here. (From the good ones this
> type of amulet is the one I wear least in my games; YMMV.)

Me too, but some people seem to like them.  As I said, it's a
trade off.  Each player has to decide what compromizes are best
for him.

> And what you write about robes doesn't even closely reflect
> the experiences I made in my games. Again, YMMV. Probability
> for robes is on the *far* lower end for random generation.
> And given that there are generally not that many temples, let
> aside that you really want to kill the priest (or can kill him
> at all[*]) that argument appears to be quite artificially
> constructed.

> [*] Sure everything is possible in principle, but that's not
> the point - the relation of difficulty compared to other
> actions is the key to judge what's easy/difficult.

My experience is that *usually*, there will be more than one
temple.  And that even if there is only one, once you've bought
all the protection the priest will give, you don't really need
him.

As for killing priests, I've recently found that relatively
trivial: tripe+polymorph trap.  You'll usually have to go
through the ritual a couple of times, but if you try, it's
really, really rare to not end up with a pet who'll take out the
priest for you.

> > And I consider attack spells almost essential for
> > disenchanters, and any monster you don't want to get too
> > close to on watery levels.

> As far as spells are very helpful against this type of monster
> I agree. Those spells are also very helpful for MF/MMFs or even
> sea creatures, if you like. But even if the spells are helpful
> (that no one will doubt, I suppose) it's *not* - you say
> "almost" - essential.

Well, my milage does vary: I find disenchanters very painful
without an attack spell.  And things like MMFs on a water level
as well.  But obviously, it depends.  One ranger I played found
the mines full of arrow traps.  When you've got something like
700 arrows, you don't mind using them, even on water levels.
That's what they're there for, after all.

There's no real hard and fast limit as to what's necessary, and
what's just for fun, but I tend to consider attack spells as an
essential part of my ascension kit.

> > > The Samurai may like to spend few XLs to become Skilled in
> > > attack spells; I'll add that to the "fun" argument. In my
> > > own games it very much depends whether I try to read
> > > spellbooks at all; it's generally too risky for many
> > > character classes (unless you take extreem precaucions,
> > > like pre-identifying or blessing a spellbook and reading
> > > it on a safe spot on a no-teleport level and having the
> > > respective stats pushed to increase the chances to read
> > > it).

> > I don't consider price-id'ing the spell books an extreme
> > precaution, and I'll also take A little bit of risk at times.

> That's not "a little bit of risk". To value it appropriately
> one has to multiply the probability of risk occurrance with
> the damage done if the risk actually occurs. Lethality is no
> acceptable damage even if the probability would have been low;
> and it isn't even low.

Maybe I've just been lucky, but I've never died because of
reading a spellbook at too low of a level.

> That risk taking insta-killed me in more than one game (call
> me stupid). If you are aware of that risk it's silly to take
> it unnecessarily.

I'm curious.  Which effect was it that insta-killed you?  (I do
make sure that there are no monsters around before reading it,
but other than that...)

> The point with the "extreem precautions" is that any omitted
> step may kill you instantly completely "helpless". Feel free
> to gamble at your desire. :-)

> If a class has to primarily rely on spells there's a different
> plan to follow than if a character is a fighter or a ranged
> combatant. In Nethack you can stress those roles, but usually
> there's a price for it. (We were focussing on Samurai here.)

Yes, but I seem to find that no matter what the character, you
need a little of everything.  My Wizards can melee to a certain
degree (maybe that's why I've never ascended one:-), and even as
a Valk or a Samurai, I do count on a few spells (cure sickness,
if I can, and attack spells for ranged attack).

> > But a high XL reduces the risk as well; at XL30, you're almost
> > certainly guaranteed to be able to read them all, as long as
> > they aren't cursed (and your pet will tell you that).  Another
> > argument for uping your experience.

> That's correct. If you want to cast spells (and need slots for
> that) the high XLs will serve both purposes. If your class does
> not need to cast spells you can skip that.

I think that's where we disagree.  I think that all classes need
some ability in spell casting, just as all need some ability in
melee fighting (with the possible exception of my ranger with
700 blessed arrows---he never put down the bow).  My own
experience is that flexibility is the key---having a variety of
solutions available to use.

> But to really make it "another argument" you have to admit
> that XL increase has to occur before you start reading books
> if you want to rely on that effect (or take any of the other
> precautions I mentioned, and then we're back at the argument I
> made). Now if you decide to enhance XL as early as possible
> you'll get also all the disadvantages early - one should be
> aware of that.

No real disagreement there.  With fighter classes like Valk or
Samurai, I'll level up fairly quickly; with others, I'll tend to
keep the XL lower.  At least in the early game: once I've gotten
to minetown, done Sobokan, and have the armor and a weapon I
consider acceptable for the middle game, I'll start leveling up
fairly quickly, regardless.

> > Which supposes a scroll of genocide.  They seem to be a lot
> > rarer than robes in my games.

> That impression is not backed up by the actual numbers, though,
> as far as I see.

I don't know.  It is just an impression, and it may be partially
due to the fact that I haven't identified the scrolls.  But as I
say, it's really rare that I don't have a robe after the castle
(and often before), where as I've done a couple of genocideless
ascensions simply because I didn't find and identify a scroll of
genocide before it became more or less academic---at a level
where even Liches didn't really scare me any more.

> > (In most games, a priest, plus a
> > pet, plus a polymorph trap results in a robe.)

> Even in games where I have spare priests to feed there's some
> effort to obtain the appropriate pet to get the priests robe.

But it's effort well spent in general.  Independently of the
robe, having a pet who'll take out the guards in minetown, or
many of the other monsters, is not to be scorned.  (I had a
water troll in my current game.  Until he ate a chameleon,
anytime things looked the least bit difficult, I just scribbled
Elbereth and waited.  He took care of the rest.)

I'll admit that I find pets kind of a drag in the early game:
trying not to loose them and all.  (Although I also find them
essential---if only to check armor before trying it on.)  But a
strong pet in the middle game is pure fun.

> And you require additional equipment, like magic whistles or
> (mainly) means of MR. Again my experience[*] does not reflect
> that this is a common scenario that you can sufficiently rely
> on[**]. But YMMV, of course.

> [*] And I am really an extreem pet-trap-whistler, if I have
> the appropriate equipment.

> [**] IME much more effective is to wait for a random purple
> worm or reverse genocide purple worms and tame them; but that
> needs some rare equipment, too.

It varies, and it's probably not something you can rely upon.
But it's also not excessively rare, and certainly worth
exploiting when the occasion presents itself.

Anyway, I've lost track of where we were, or what we were
arguing about.  My only real point is that I value flexibility,
and being able to cast spells gives me an added flexibility,
even in fighter classes, and if there's any possibility of
having it, I'll go for it.  It's not "just for fun" (except that
in the sense that anything we do in the game is for fun).

